, {"step_id": "step_13", "tool": "burst_detection", "args": {"nodes": [384, 716, 946, 948, 1116, 1121, 1304, 1464, 1572, 1635, 1740, 1955], "window": [33806, 63806], "n_bins": 15, "z_threshold": 1.5}, "result_digest": "c7b55c80214c2919", "result_summary": "{\"bursts\": [], \"mean\": 73.07, \"n_bursts\": 0, \"sd\": 22.19, \"z_threshold\": 1.5}", "latency_ms": 1, "tokens_before": 38241, "tokens_after": 38241, "ok": true, "error": ""}, {"step_id": "step_14", "tool": "compute", "args": {"expression": "1 - peak_change", "values": {"peak_change": 0.2857}}, "result_digest": "7fd0f42323d2c96b", "result_summary": "{\"expression\": \"1 - peak_change\", \"result\": 0.7142999999999999}", "latency_ms": 0, "tokens_before": 49870, "tokens_after": 49870, "ok": true, "error": ""}], "final_claim": {"task_id": "t_459f9d4b", "decision": "absent", "mechanism": "regime_switch", "entities": [384, 716, 946, 948, 1116, 1121, 1304, 1464, 1572, 1635, 1740, 1955], "window": [33806, 63806], "confidence": 0.85, "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_10", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "No regime switch in the group's contact partners. At first glance the data looks like a textbook switch: edge-level diffs across candidate change points are near-total (step_03 Jaccard 0.15; step_04 and step_10 Jaccard 0.00, with ~60-92 edges removed and a comparable number added). But the added/removed samples reveal that the churn is entirely on the group side of each edge, not the partner side. In step_03 the removed edges are 716->{98,117,204,205,258,...} and 946->{same list}, while the added edges are 1116->{98,117,204,205,258,...} and 1121->{the identical list}. The same substitution repeats in step_04 (1116/1121 out, 1304/1464 in) and step_10 (384/716 out, 1635/1740 in), always against the same recurring roster of ~30 external nodes.\n\nDirect inspection of the contact sets confirms partner stability end-to-end: node 384's neighbours in the first 10k units (step_08) and node 1955's neighbours in the last 10k units (step_09) share 30 of 31 nodes, a Jaccard of 0.97 (step_11), differing only by the singleton 382. Nodes 1116 (step_05) and 1464 (step_12) draw on that same roster. So an old set of contacts never gives way to a new set; the external neighbourhood is essentially constant while the baton of activity passes between group members (step_06: 384/716/946/948 carry ~90 events each early with 1572/1635/1740/1955 silent; step_07: exactly the reverse late).\n\nThe turnover series is also the wrong shape for an abrupt transition: step_01 shows a low, broad plateau around 0.27 with a weak maximum of 0.2857 at t=42908, decaying smoothly to 0.03 rather than spiking at a change point \u2014 and even that peak is driven by member rotation, since the listed before/after contact sets overlap in all but a handful of nodes. Volume-based explanations are ruled out as well: activity is flat near the 73-event mean with no bursts at z>=1.5 (step_13), and the one anomaly in step_02 is an empty bin at 47806-49805, a quiescent gap rather than a pivot to new partners. The signal is member rotation over a fixed audience, not a regime switch."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_459f9d4b\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [384, 716, 946, 948, 1116, 1121, 1304, 1464, 1572, 1635, 1740, 1955],\n  \"window\": [33806, 63806],\n  \"confidence\": 0.85,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"No regime switch in the group's contact partners. At first glance the data looks like a textbook switch: edge-level diffs across candidate change points are near-total (step_03 Jaccard 0.15; step_04 and step_10 Jaccard 0.00, with ~60-92 edges removed and a comparable number added). But the added/removed samples reveal that the churn is entirely on the group side of each edge, not the partner side. In step_03 the removed edges are 716->{98,117,204,205,258,...} and 946->{same list}, while the added edges are 1116->{98,117,204,205,258,...} and 1121->{the identical list}. The same substitution repeats in step_04 (1116/1121 out, 1304/1464 in) and step_10 (384/716 out, 1635/1740 in), always against the same recurring roster of ~30 external nodes.\\n\\nDirect inspection of the contact sets confirms partner stability end-to-end: node 384's neighbours in the first 10k units (step_08) and node 1955's neighbours in the last 10k units (step_09) share 30 of 31 nodes, a Jaccard of 0.97 (step_11), differing only by the singleton 382. Nodes 1116 (step_05) and 1464 (step_12) draw on that same roster. So an old set of contacts never gives way to a new set; the external neighbourhood is essentially constant while the baton of activity passes between group members (step_06: 384/716/946/948 carry ~90 events each early with 1572/1635/1740/1955 silent; step_07: exactly the reverse late).\\n\\nThe turnover series is also the wrong shape for an abrupt transition: step_01 shows a low, broad plateau around 0.27 with a weak maximum of 0.2857 at t=42908, decaying smoothly to 0.03 rather than spiking at a change point \u2014 and even that peak is driven by member rotation, since the listed before/after contact sets overlap in all but a handful of nodes. Volume-based explanations are ruled out as well: activity is flat near the 73-event mean with no bursts at z>=1.5 (step_13), and the one anomaly in step_02 is an empty bin at 47806-49805, a quiescent gap rather than a pivot to new partners. The signal is member rotation over a fixed audience, not a regime switch.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 61663, "total_output_tokens": 5255, "total_tokens": 66918, "total_latency_ms": 75061, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}